# Warm transfer out
#
# The assistant hands the caller to a human agent in the Everyone queue, staying on the
# line to introduce them before it leaves. Picks up where direct-answer-call.d2 ends,
# with the caller and the assistant already on a conference.
#
# Code: controllers/event-openai-post.go (startWarmTransfer, awaitAgentJoin)
#       controllers/taskrouter-queue-assignment-callback-post.go (joinWorkerToConference)
#       controllers/event-conference-post.go
#       services/warm-transfer/broker.go

title: |md
  # Warm transfer a call
  The assistant stays with the caller until an agent joins, introduces them, then leaves.
| {near: top-center}

shape: sequence_diagram

caller: Caller
voice: Twilio Voice and Conference
taskrouter: Twilio TaskRouter
app: zenn-ai-assistant
openai: OpenAI Realtime API
agent: Human agent

# Precondition: caller and assistant are already on a conference named after the task sid.

caller -> openai: Asks to speak to a person
openai -> app: response.function_call_arguments.done (transfer_call with target_queue and summary)

queue_task: A task is queued for the Everyone queue {
  app -> taskrouter: Finds the task by the assistant's call sid
  app -> taskrouter: Resolves the Everyone queue by friendly name
  app -> app: Watches the conference BEFORE the task exists

  # An agent can answer faster than CreateTask returns. Registering the watch first is
  # what stops the join signal being missed.

  app -> taskrouter: Creates a task carrying conference_name, conference_sid and the summary
}

reassure: The caller is told what is happening {
  app -> openai: conversation.item.create (function_call_output) then response.create
  openai -> caller: Tells the caller an agent is coming and that it will stay on the line
}

pickup: An agent picks the task up {
  taskrouter -> taskrouter: Reserves a worker in the Everyone queue
  taskrouter -> app: POST /twilio/taskrouter/queue-assignment-callback
  app -> app: Task attributes say is_warm_transfer, so this is a transfer reservation
  app -> taskrouter: instruction accept

  # Not a conference instruction. That would name a NEW conference after the new task
  # sid, when the caller is already sitting in the original one.

  app -> voice: Adds the worker to the EXISTING conference, end_conference_on_exit true
  voice -> agent: Rings the agent
  agent -> voice: Answers and joins the conference
}

signal: The assistant learns the agent has arrived {
  voice -> app: POST /twilio/conference/event (participant-join)
  app -> app: Signals the broker watching this conference
}

handoff: The warm moment - all three are on the call {
  app -> app: Marks the transfer handed off, so end_call now means leave
  app -> openai: response.create to introduce the caller with the summary
  openai -> agent: Speaks the handoff summary
  agent -> openai: Asks any follow-up questions
  openai -> agent: Answers them

  # The assistant stays on the call after the introduction. Finishing the introduction is
  # not the same as being finished, and the agent usually has questions.
}

leave: The assistant decides it is done {
  openai -> app: response.function_call_arguments.done (end_call)
  app -> taskrouter: Completes the assistant's task
  app -> openai: POST /v1/realtime/calls/ID/hangup
  openai -> voice: Assistant leg leaves, end_conference_on_exit is false
  caller -> agent: Caller and agent stay connected

  # end_call does NOT end the conference here. The caller and the agent are still talking
  # on it, so the assistant only drops its own leg.
}

waiting: If no agent joins within two minutes {
  app -> openai: response.create to check back in with the caller
  openai -> caller: Says it is still holding and asks if they want to keep waiting
  app -> app: Keeps waiting - the task stays queued
}
